<ArticleLayout Sections="Sections" Path="job-shop">
    <section id="problem">
        <h2>The problem</h2>
        <p>
            A workshop has several machines and a list of jobs. Each job is a fixed sequence of <strong>operations</strong>, and each operation needs one
            specific machine for a known time: a bike frame is cut, then welded, then painted. A machine works on one operation at a time, an operation
            can't be interrupted once started, and a job's operations must run in order. Find a schedule that finishes everything as early as possible.
            The finishing time of the last operation is the <strong>makespan</strong>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Factories are the classic setting, but the same structure appears whenever tasks with an internal order compete for shared resources: operating
            rooms and surgical teams, test rigs in a lab, compute jobs on a cluster, or a print shop where every order passes through different presses.
            With due dates added, the goal often shifts from makespan to lateness.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section id="model">
        <h2>Formal model</h2>
        <p>Give each operation <i>o</i> a start time <i>S<sub>o</sub></i> and duration <i>p<sub>o</sub></i>. Then:</p>
        <div class="formula" role="math" aria-label="job shop constraints">
            <div class="row"><span>minimize</span><span><i>C</i><sub>max</sub> ≥ <i>S<sub>o</sub></i> + <i>p<sub>o</sub></i> for every operation <i>o</i></span></div>
            <div class="row"><span>job order</span><span><i>S<sub>j,k+1</sub></i> ≥ <i>S<sub>j,k</sub></i> + <i>p<sub>j,k</sub></i></span></div>
            <div class="row"><span>one at a time</span><span><i>S<sub>o</sub></i> ≥ <i>S<sub>o′</sub></i> + <i>p<sub>o′</sub></i> &nbsp;or&nbsp; <i>S<sub>o′</sub></i> ≥ <i>S<sub>o</sub></i> + <i>p<sub>o</sub></i> &nbsp;when <i>o</i>, <i>o′</i> share a machine</span></div>
        </div>
        <p>
            That "or" is what makes it hard, and it has a beautiful picture: the <strong>disjunctive graph</strong>. Every operation is a node. Arrows
            fixed by job order are always there; every pair of operations on the same machine is joined by an undirected edge that must be turned into an
            arrow one way or the other. Pick a direction for every such edge without creating a cycle and you've picked a schedule. Its makespan is the
            length of the <strong>longest path</strong> through the graph, which is exactly the critical path the lab highlights in amber.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section id="hard">
        <h2>Why it's hard</h2>
        <p>
            A schedule is fixed by choosing the order of operations on each machine. With <i>n</i> jobs that each visit all <i>m</i> machines, that's
            (<i>n</i>!)<sup><i>m</i></sup> combinations: about 1.4 × 10<sup>17</sup> for the 6 × 6 instance ft06, and 4 × 10<sup>65</sup> for its
            10 × 10 sibling ft10. Many are infeasible, many are equivalent, but no known method avoids exponential effort in the worst case. The problem is
            strongly NP-hard.
        </p>
        <p>
            ft10 was published in 1963 and its optimum, 930, wasn't proven until 1989. Benchmark instances from the early 1990s still include some
            whose optimum nobody has proven. Job-shop scheduling is famous for being hard in practice, not just in theory.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section id="structures">
        <h2>Data structures</h2>
        <h3>The disjunctive graph as arrays</h3>
        <p>
            Tabu search stores a schedule as one array per machine listing its operations in order. Together with each job's fixed order, that defines a
            directed acyclic graph. A topological sort (Kahn's algorithm, using an in-degree count and a queue) visits the operations so that each
            one's predecessors are done first, and the earliest start of each is the larger of its job predecessor's and machine predecessor's finish times.
            That's the longest path, computed in <i>O</i>(<i>N</i>) for <i>N</i> operations. If the sort can't visit every node, the orders contain a cycle
            and the schedule is impossible.
        </p>
        <h3>Ready times and a waiting set</h3>
        <p>
            Dispatching keeps, for every job and every machine, the time it becomes free, plus the set of operations whose job predecessor is done.
            Each round computes earliest start and finish for every waiting operation, so a round costs <i>O</i>(<i>n</i>) and the whole schedule
            <i>O</i>(<i>n</i>² · <i>m</i>).
        </p>
        <h3>The tabu list</h3>
        <p>
            Tabu search's short-term memory is a hash map from an ordered pair of operations to the iteration until which swapping them back is forbidden.
            Checking a move is a single lookup.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section id="algorithms">
        <h2>The algorithms</h2>
        <h3>Giffler–Thompson with priority rules</h3>
        <p>
            The Giffler–Thompson algorithm builds an <strong>active</strong> schedule, one where no operation could start earlier without delaying another.
            Some optimal schedule is always active, so the method never rules out the best answer by construction. In each round it finds the waiting operation
            that could finish first, looks at its machine, and collects every operation that wants that machine and could start before that finish time.
            Only those genuinely compete; a <strong>priority rule</strong> picks the winner:
        </p>
        <table class="compare-table">
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Rule</th><th>Picks</th><th>Intuition</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>MWKR</td><td>Most work remaining</td><td>Keep the longest job moving; it is the one most likely to set the makespan</td></tr>
                <tr><td>SPT</td><td>Shortest operation</td><td>Clear quick work first; good for average flow time</td></tr>
                <tr><td>LPT</td><td>Longest operation</td><td>Get big blocks out of the way early</td></tr>
                <tr><td>LWKR</td><td>Least work remaining</td><td>Finish nearly-done jobs</td></tr>
                <tr><td>FCFS</td><td>Earliest ready</td><td>Fair queueing, no lookahead</td></tr>
                <tr><td>EDD</td><td>Earliest due date</td><td>Fight lateness rather than makespan</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>
            None of them is best on every instance, which is why the comparison chart is worth a look on each example.
        </p>
        <h3>Tabu search on the critical path</h3>
        <p>
            Only the critical path determines the makespan, so only reordering operations on it can shorten the schedule. The neighborhood used here,
            from van Laarhoven, Aarts and Lenstra, swaps two adjacent operations that share a machine on the critical path. Such a swap can never
            create a cycle, so every neighbor is a valid schedule. Each iteration moves to the best neighbor, even if it is worse, and forbids undoing the move for a few
            iterations. That forced exploration is what lets it climb out of local optima. On ft06 it reaches the proven optimum of 55.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section id="beyond">
        <h2>Beyond this lab</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Constraint programming.</strong> Today's strongest exact solvers, such as Google's CP-SAT and IBM's CP Optimizer, reason about time windows on each machine and prune huge parts of the search with clever propagation.</li>
            <li><strong>Shifting bottleneck.</strong> Adams, Balas and Zawack's 1988 heuristic schedules the most constrained machine first by solving a one-machine problem, then reoptimizes the others.</li>
            <li><strong>Stronger tabu search.</strong> Nowicki and Smutnicki's 1996 algorithm restricts swaps to the ends of critical blocks, which cuts the neighborhood without losing good moves. It was state of the art for years.</li>
            <li><strong>Variants.</strong> Flow shop, where every job visits the machines in the same order (Johnson's 1954 rule solves the two-machine case exactly); open shop, where the order is free; flexible job shop, where an operation can run on any of several machines; and versions with setup times or due dates.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="history">
        <h2>A little history</h2>
        <p>
            Selmer Johnson solved the two-machine flow shop in 1954, one of the first results in scheduling theory. Bernard Giffler and Gerald Thompson
            described active schedules and how to generate them in 1960. In 1963 Henry Fisher and Gerald Thompson published the test problems now called
            ft06, ft10 and ft20, and in 1964 Bernard Roy and B. Sussmann introduced the disjunctive graph. Jacques Carlier and Éric Pinson finally proved
            ft10's optimum with branch and bound in 1989.
        </p>
    </section>
</ArticleLayout>

